    "title": "pandas.PeriodIndex.asfreq — pandas 3.1.0.dev0 documentation",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "# pandas.PeriodIndex.asfreq#\n\n- \nPeriodIndex.asfreq(*freq=None* ,*how='E'* )[source]#\n- Convert the PeriodIndex to the specified frequency freq. Equivalent to applying `pandas.Period.asfreq()` with the given arguments\nto each`Period` in this PeriodIndex.\n  - Parameters:\n    - **freq** str\n    - A frequency.\n    - **how** str {‘E’, ‘S’}, default ‘E’\n    - Whether the elements should be aligned to the end or start within pa period. \n      - ‘E’, ‘END’, or ‘FINISH’ for end,\n      - ‘S’, ‘START’, or ‘BEGIN’ for start.\n January 31st (‘END’) vs. January 1st (‘START’) for example.\n  - Returns:\n    - PeriodIndex\n    - The transformed PeriodIndex with the new frequency.\n See also \n  - `arrays.PeriodArray.asfreq`\n  - Convert each Period in a PeriodArray to the given frequency.\n  - `Period.asfreq`\n  - Convert a `Period` object to the given frequency.\n Examples >>> pidx = pd.period_range(\"2010-01-01\", \"2015-01-01\", freq=\"Y\") >>> pidx PeriodIndex(['2010', '2011', '2012', '2013', '2014', '2015'], dtype='period[Y-DEC]') >>> pidx.asfreq(\"M\") PeriodIndex(['2010-12', '2011-12', '2012-12', '2013-12', '2014-12', '2015-12'], dtype='period[M]') >>> pidx.asfreq(\"M\", how=\"S\") PeriodIndex(['2010-01', '2011-01', '2012-01', '2013-01', '2014-01', '2015-01'], dtype='period[M]')",
    "markdown_length": 1269,
